    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d114">

                <pb facs="0374" n="358" xml:id="pg358"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Policy Planning Staff Files</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Mr. Carlton Savage, Member of the Policy
                        Planning Staff, to Mr. Paul H. Nitze, Director of the Policy Planning
                        Staff</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1950-08-03">August 3, 1950</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The attached paper concerning the conditions which might call for a U.S.
                    declaration of war has a direct bearing on the questions of mobilization and
                    execution of war plans. It seems to me that the subject is appropriate for
                    consideration in connection with them.</p>
                <p>The paper embodies suggestions made by George Kennan and he concurs in the
                    conclusions and recommendations. The legal aspects of the paper were gone over
                    by Mr. Yingling, a senior officer of “L”, and it embodies some suggestions made
                    by him.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>C[<hi rendition="#small-caps">arlton</hi>] S[<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >avage</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d114-a1.xml" xpointer="d114-a1"/>
            </div>
